WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.604 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.972
-Is this my camera?

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.408
[ Laughter ]

00:00:09.408 --> 00:00:10.877
And we're starting?

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.215
Good evening.
This is "Corrections"

00:00:16.215 --> 00:00:17.850
for the week of February 21st.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.119
Filling in for Seth Meyers,
I'm Lewis Forsyth.

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:22.121
I'm [bleep] with you guys.

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:24.490
[ Laughter ]

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.425
It's me.

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:28.862
It was me the whole time. Whoa.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.463
[ Laughter ]

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:33.066
I wish you could have
seen your faces.

00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:35.601
And then you could have told me
what they looked like,

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:38.671
'cause I couldn't
see them, either.

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:42.609
We had some fun with the mask.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:45.812
But the mask isn't always fun.

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:50.182
The mask, which I wore
at our last "Corrections,"

00:00:50.182 --> 00:00:53.452
cost me my relationship...

00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:56.122
with Pizza Hut.

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.458
We've obviously
talked about this.

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:04.330
I was gifted the slogan
"No one out-pizzas the Hut."

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.732
Which was a pretty big deal --
pretty excited about it.

00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:10.102
But then I wore that mask,
and the bigwigs --

00:01:10.102 --> 00:01:13.539
the brass over at Pizza Hut...

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:15.675
decided that
when I wore the mask,

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:19.178
I looked too much like the Noid.

00:01:19.178 --> 00:01:21.614
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.683
Which is a...

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:26.152
Which is a Domino's mascot.

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:28.287
And they called up pretty angry,
and they said,

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:31.090
"That was the one thing...

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.660
we asked you to avoid."

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.328
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.903
Buckle up.

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:47.406
Again, a lot
of these corrections

00:01:47.406 --> 00:01:51.244
are multiple corrections deep,
at this point.

00:01:52.911 --> 00:01:56.515
We called one piece of confetti
a "confettum."

00:01:56.515 --> 00:02:00.987
We were corrected by many of you
that it was "confetto."

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:05.358
One piece of confetti
is a confetto.

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:08.360
I said I was so impressed
and remarked --

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.163
complimented, even --
the Jackals

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:12.098
for knowing their Latin so well.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:15.235
Then one of you had the guts
to come clean and say

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:19.772
you all knew that
because it was in an episode

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:22.208
of "The Big Bang Theory."

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:26.445
And when you think
people know something

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:29.348
because they know Latin...

00:02:29.348 --> 00:02:32.785
and you find out it's because
they watched "The Bang"...

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:37.656
It's a big difference, guys.

00:02:37.656 --> 00:02:39.325
I myself have never watched
"Big Bang Theory,"

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.927
so I didn't know that from that.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.129
I don't know the inside jokes.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:45.031
I don't know the catchphrases.

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:48.267
But I'm impressed that
you learned a little Latin

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:49.535
from watching it.

00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:52.205
Speaking of Latin,
I took a risk last time out...

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:55.975
...and I got burned.

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:57.943
If there's any rule
to "Corrections,"

00:02:57.943 --> 00:03:00.146
it's don't try to say
a word you're not sure of

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:03.116
in the body of "Corrections."

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:05.051
And I threw out...

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:07.420
"remunerations."

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.822
Pronounced it "renumerations."

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.492
And that was wrong.

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:14.827
I thought "num,"

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:17.329
'cause it sounded like "number,"

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:20.333
but a Jackal explained it comes
from the Latin "munis,"

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:22.935
which means
"gift, duty, or office."

00:03:23.936 --> 00:03:27.072
Which seem like
three different things.

00:03:27.072 --> 00:03:30.709
Seems like "munis" shouldn't be
in all three of those things.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:32.378
As the host of this show,
every Christmas,

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:36.749
I feel like it's my duty to get
a gift for the whole office.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:39.285
Or, in Latin,
"munis munis munis."

00:03:43.355 --> 00:03:45.892
Baze, you backed me up...

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.827
when I said "renumerations."

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:52.332
Also, people always say,
"Is Baze short for --"

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:53.899
They ask if it's a nickname.

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:55.734
It is short -- for "Bazinga."

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:56.903
I have watched.

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:59.172
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.611 --> 00:04:07.113
I also joked that
"Confetto" was the name

00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:09.749
of the fifth Ninja Turtle
who liked to party.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:11.784
That opened a whole can
of Jackals.

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:15.921
Michelangelo was the Turtle
who liked to party.

00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:19.157
And I said, "How could you
possibly know that?"

00:04:19.157 --> 00:04:21.593
And I was told that...

00:04:21.593 --> 00:04:24.763
in the opening song...

00:04:24.763 --> 00:04:28.334
there's the lyric
"Michelangelo is a party dude."

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:34.040
I then watch.
I wanted to back it up, right?

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.707
So I watched the opening credits

00:04:35.707 --> 00:04:39.512
to the "Teenage Mutant
Ninja Turtles" cartoon.

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:42.247
And I hate to be a Jackal...

00:04:42.247 --> 00:04:44.817
to that,
but I got some problems.

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:47.920
Some of the lyrical choices.

00:04:50.222 --> 00:04:53.458
There's a line,
"When the evil Shredder attacks,

00:04:53.458 --> 00:04:55.061
these turtles boy --"

00:04:55.061 --> 00:04:58.831
"These turtle boys
don't cut him no slack."

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:02.268
Double negative, first off,
right off the bat.

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:03.903
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.637 --> 00:05:07.740
"Don't cut him no slack"?

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.809
Well, that's an expression
kids use.

00:05:13.178 --> 00:05:14.480
There was a fifth Turtle.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.316
There was a fifth Turtle --
a female Turtle.

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:20.319
There were two different ones --
Venus de Milo in the show,

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:23.422
Jennika -- hope I'm saying
that, right -- in the comics.

00:05:23.422 --> 00:05:26.225
So I apologize
for that Turtle erasure.

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:29.261
I also talked about the masks.

00:05:30.429 --> 00:05:33.232
Do you really think
a turtle needs to wear a mask

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:35.334
to disguise their identity?

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:37.403
Many of you said, "Oh, no."

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:40.440
It was to tell
the turtles apart.

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:44.510
But then someone else said,
"Not originally, though."

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.213
Originally,
they all wore red masks,

00:05:47.213 --> 00:05:49.982
and it wasn't until they became
popularized in the cartoons

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.617
that they switched
the colors of the masks

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:55.754
because then that would
make the toys...

00:05:55.754 --> 00:05:56.955
easier to tell apart.

00:05:56.955 --> 00:06:00.293
And, so, like the answer
to most questions...

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.895
...that start with "why"...

00:06:03.895 --> 00:06:05.465
the answer is capitalism.

00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:09.335
Put that in there for you, Sal.

00:06:09.335 --> 00:06:11.070
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.871 --> 00:06:14.607
Hey, I talked about when we had
a sloth on the show,

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:17.576
and I said -- and it was about

00:06:17.576 --> 00:06:19.545
why we don't have animals
on the show

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:23.616
and how the sloth
was desperately trying

00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:26.351
to make eye contact with me
to show in its eyes

00:06:26.351 --> 00:06:27.954
that it didn't want to be here.

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:31.624
And I said that as it turned --
as it slowly turned to face me,

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:35.461
I saw sweat running down
its temple.

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:39.098
And I was told
sloths don't sweat.

00:06:39.098 --> 00:06:41.567
They don't sweat because
that would attract predators,

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.669
and you don't need to know
much about sloths

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:46.271
to know that the minute
a predator knows where you are

00:06:46.271 --> 00:06:49.108
as a sloth, game over.

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:51.110
[ Laughter ]

00:06:52.978 --> 00:06:54.713
I was told
the two-fingered sloth

00:06:54.713 --> 00:06:58.217
sweats a tiny bit
through their nose,

00:06:58.217 --> 00:07:00.920
and that's how you can tell
when they're lying.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:06.058
And they know,
so they will try to cover it.

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:11.230
So, like, a sloth would say,
"Did I eat your Baby Ruth?"

00:07:12.397 --> 00:07:14.667
[ Laughter ]
"No."

00:07:17.903 --> 00:07:20.605
Also, I did talk
about the sloth,

00:07:20.605 --> 00:07:23.242
and I said "he" a lot.

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:25.644
The sloth was not a "he."
Somebody went back and watched.

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:27.579
It was a she,
and her name was Serenity.

00:07:27.579 --> 00:07:29.582
And this is interesting --
they named her that

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:31.517
because she was also a stripper.

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:33.619
[ Laughter ]

00:07:34.753 --> 00:07:37.523
I guess more burlesque.

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:40.392
And I'm told if you want
to go see her dance,

00:07:40.392 --> 00:07:43.529
you gotta carve out some time.

00:07:43.529 --> 00:07:46.332
It's a long routine.

00:07:46.332 --> 00:07:49.134
It's a long --
I guess it's very alluring.

00:07:49.134 --> 00:07:51.336
It's very coy.

00:07:51.336 --> 00:07:52.838
But it takes --

00:07:52.838 --> 00:07:54.907
Start to finish,
it takes a while.

00:07:56.208 --> 00:07:58.277
It's not --

00:07:58.277 --> 00:08:01.547
It's very hard on the deejays,
I've heard.

00:08:01.547 --> 00:08:05.017
Because it's not
a one-song situation.

00:08:05.017 --> 00:08:06.786
It's, like, full albums.

00:08:11.256 --> 00:08:13.926
"And now..."
[ Laughter ]

00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:17.363
"Here to perform to Bob Dylan's
'Blonde on Blonde...'"

00:08:18.964 --> 00:08:21.600
"...give it up for Serenity."

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.736
I guess guys
fall in love with her.

00:08:25.004 --> 00:08:26.472
It happens.

00:08:26.472 --> 00:08:28.908
I don't go to those
kind of establishments,

00:08:28.908 --> 00:08:31.243
but I hear that sometimes --

00:08:31.243 --> 00:08:35.881
And then, I guess, you know --
I'll be in the champagne room,

00:08:35.881 --> 00:08:37.316
or whatever it is
they call them.

00:08:37.316 --> 00:08:38.818
Again, these are all things I've
gleaned from popular culture.

00:08:38.818 --> 00:08:40.319
I've never been...

00:08:42.254 --> 00:08:45.090
And guys will say, like,
"Serenity, do you love me

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:46.458
as much as I love you?"

00:08:46.458 --> 00:08:48.661
And she'll say, "Um..."

00:08:48.661 --> 00:08:50.429
[ Laughter ]

00:08:55.267 --> 00:08:56.668
This is a self-correction.

00:08:56.668 --> 00:08:59.938
Nobody called this out.
I'm calling this out on myself.

00:08:59.938 --> 00:09:04.143
So, we had Billie Eilish
on the show last night.

00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:06.879
Wonderful, wonderful person.

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:08.480
Wonderful family.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.583
And there are certain guests --

00:09:10.583 --> 00:09:12.751
It doesn't happen often
on this show.

00:09:12.751 --> 00:09:14.019
There are certain guests

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:17.389
that change the demographic
of our audience.

00:09:17.389 --> 00:09:20.993
And she has a very fervent,
very lovely fan base.

00:09:20.993 --> 00:09:23.729
And you got the sense,
as I walked out last night,

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:25.797
this is younger.

00:09:25.797 --> 00:09:29.368
A younger audience
than we're accustomed to.

00:09:29.368 --> 00:09:32.271
It did not make any part of
the show less enjoyable for me.

00:09:32.271 --> 00:09:33.872
If anything,
it was more enjoyable

00:09:33.872 --> 00:09:36.241
to have their energy
in the house.

00:09:36.241 --> 00:09:39.411
But what I'm correcting myself
is, I knew she was here.

00:09:39.411 --> 00:09:41.681
It's not like I find out
when the show starts

00:09:41.681 --> 00:09:43.716
that Billie Eilish is here.

00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:46.184
And yet, I personally inserted

00:09:46.184 --> 00:09:50.522
a "Welcome Back, Kotter" joke
into "A Closer Look."

00:09:50.522 --> 00:09:52.891
Which then, you know...

00:09:52.891 --> 00:09:54.359
played the way
you would think it would

00:09:54.359 --> 00:09:56.495
in a room full of
Billie Eilish fans.

00:09:56.495 --> 00:09:58.130
But then I got a note...

00:10:00.432 --> 00:10:02.867
...from a Jackal that said...

00:10:02.867 --> 00:10:05.504
I hope you don't mind.

00:10:06.572 --> 00:10:08.507
"Dear Seth, even though your
'Welcome Back, Kotter' joke

00:10:08.507 --> 00:10:11.177
didn't get an in-studio reaction
last night, I want you to know

00:10:11.177 --> 00:10:12.677
I laughed very hard at home.

00:10:12.677 --> 00:10:16.815
This came at the end
of a very hard day for me.

00:10:16.815 --> 00:10:17.883
I want to say thank you,

00:10:17.883 --> 00:10:20.285
and keep doing the jokes
you believe in.

00:10:20.285 --> 00:10:22.655
Signed, Epstein's mother."

00:10:22.655 --> 00:10:24.757
[ Laughter ]

00:10:30.629 --> 00:10:32.631
Some quick hits.
I said "guidance counselor."

00:10:32.631 --> 00:10:36.701
I was told by someone who has
the job now they provide --

00:10:36.701 --> 00:10:39.238
they prefer, excuse me,
the term "school counselor,"

00:10:39.238 --> 00:10:42.041
as they provide a great deal
more than guidance these days.

00:10:42.041 --> 00:10:44.343
E.R.'s.
I think we say "E.R." a lot.

00:10:44.343 --> 00:10:47.012
I guess they're now
more commonly known as E.D.'s,

00:10:47.012 --> 00:10:48.414
as they are rarely
emergency rooms,

00:10:48.414 --> 00:10:51.517
but rather entire
emergency departments,

00:10:51.517 --> 00:10:56.155
which is only a problem when
you go for a Viagra overdose.

00:10:56.155 --> 00:10:58.557
[ Laughter ]

00:10:58.557 --> 00:11:00.659
"What brings you to the E.D.?"
"E.D."

00:11:00.659 --> 00:11:02.561
[ Laughter ]

00:11:02.561 --> 00:11:04.262
"That's what I said."
"That's why I'm here."

00:11:04.262 --> 00:11:06.098
It's a real
"Who's on First" situation.

00:11:06.098 --> 00:11:07.833
[ Laughter ]

00:11:10.235 --> 00:11:12.304
[As Abbott and Costello]
"Why you in the E.D.?" "E.D.!"

00:11:12.304 --> 00:11:14.006
"Why is it so hard?!"

00:11:14.006 --> 00:11:16.775
"That's what I wanna
ask the doctor!"

00:11:16.775 --> 00:11:18.678
[ Laughter ]

00:11:27.286 --> 00:11:29.722
I referred
to "my wife's niece," Becky.

00:11:29.722 --> 00:11:31.757
A lot of you said
she would also be my niece.

00:11:31.757 --> 00:11:33.459
Nope.

00:11:33.459 --> 00:11:35.494
Not my blood. Not my problem.

00:11:35.494 --> 00:11:38.430
[ Laughter ]

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.066
Go with God, Becky.

00:11:43.702 --> 00:11:47.439
I did my Pacino impression
the other night.

00:11:47.439 --> 00:11:49.775
Yesterday. Did a line --

00:11:49.775 --> 00:11:51.943
Did a line from
"And Justice for All,"

00:11:51.943 --> 00:11:55.614
and someone pointed out
the problem was

00:11:55.614 --> 00:11:57.149
that's a '70s movie

00:11:57.149 --> 00:12:00.919
and you did
"Scent of a Woman" Pacino.

00:12:00.919 --> 00:12:04.723
And that was the only problem.

00:12:04.723 --> 00:12:08.293
That and a room full
of Billie Eilish fans.

00:12:08.293 --> 00:12:10.095
Billie Eilish fans
don't get Pacino,

00:12:10.095 --> 00:12:13.432
and I'm sure
he doesn't get them.

00:12:13.432 --> 00:12:16.301
[as Al Pacino]
"Billie Eilish?!

00:12:16.301 --> 00:12:18.170
The ballet kid?!"

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:20.339
[ Laughter ]

00:12:23.108 --> 00:12:24.610
Hey, not everybody
loves "Corrections,"

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:26.378
and I don't want
to just sit here and, you know,

00:12:26.378 --> 00:12:28.881
make this a celebration
each week.

00:12:30.949 --> 00:12:34.186
Someone said in the comments
that these "boomer rants"

00:12:34.186 --> 00:12:36.188
are so long and boring.

00:12:36.188 --> 00:12:40.692
I'm sorry to inform you, bro.
I'm Gen X.

00:12:40.692 --> 00:12:42.227
I'm Gen X.

00:12:42.227 --> 00:12:43.962
You want to talk to a boomer?
Call up my dad.

00:12:43.962 --> 00:12:45.898
You want a boomer rant?

00:12:45.898 --> 00:12:48.133
He'll happily give you an earful

00:12:48.133 --> 00:12:50.402
about how "Corrections"
are long and boring.

00:12:50.402 --> 00:12:52.304
[ Laughter ]

00:12:53.738 --> 00:12:55.908
Someone else wrote -- And this
one was even more hurtful

00:12:55.908 --> 00:12:57.509
because the first one
I feel like maybe

00:12:57.509 --> 00:13:00.078
never loved "Corrections."

00:13:00.078 --> 00:13:02.014
Someone said,
"These are starting to stink.

00:13:02.014 --> 00:13:03.849
Maybe rehearse."

00:13:03.849 --> 00:13:05.851
Uh, rehearse?

00:13:05.851 --> 00:13:07.786
[ Laughter ]

00:13:07.786 --> 00:13:10.923
You want me to put the crew
through this twice?

00:13:10.923 --> 00:13:12.792
[ Laughter ]

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:18.330
This is already so close
to an H.R. violation.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:23.868
We've talked about how we have
an anniversary show,

00:13:23.868 --> 00:13:26.238
"A Year of Corrections,"
coming up.

00:13:26.238 --> 00:13:32.110
No progress has been made.
Lower those expectations.

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:36.881
I would like to have merch
available by then.

00:13:36.881 --> 00:13:38.450
I just don't know
who to talk to.

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:42.154
I don't know who here
is in charge of that.

00:13:42.154 --> 00:13:43.889
I asked Paul,
and he told me to [bleep] off.

00:13:43.889 --> 00:13:45.524
[ Laughter ]

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:55.300
True story.

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:58.003
Tonight is the eighth
anniversary of "Late Night."

00:13:58.003 --> 00:14:02.875
And it's a little bittersweet
because Shoemaker --

00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:05.310
You might have heard the absence

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:06.845
of one of our
"Corrections" laughers.

00:14:06.845 --> 00:14:08.980
Shoemaker is not here

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:12.183
because during
our Olympics hiatus,

00:14:12.183 --> 00:14:14.452
he slipped on the ice
outside his house,

00:14:14.452 --> 00:14:16.888
and he fell
and he broke his leg.

00:14:16.888 --> 00:14:20.259
And he's doing fine.
He'll be back soon.

00:14:20.259 --> 00:14:23.495
But this place
very much does not feel

00:14:23.495 --> 00:14:26.765
like the home that it has become
over the last eight years

00:14:26.765 --> 00:14:29.000
without Shoemaker here.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.103
His wife was the one
who called me, and I said,

00:14:31.103 --> 00:14:33.138
"How did it happen?
How did he slip on the ice?"

00:14:33.138 --> 00:14:35.440
And she said,

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:38.143
"We were watching
Olympic bobsledding,

00:14:38.143 --> 00:14:39.411
and he just looked at me
and said,

00:14:39.411 --> 00:14:42.647
'These bobsledders think
they're hot [bleep].'"

00:14:42.647 --> 00:14:46.085
And I was like, "Oh, no.

00:14:46.085 --> 00:14:48.554
Did he try to bobsled
down your driveway?"

00:14:48.554 --> 00:14:51.056
And she was like,
"Yeah. He, like --

00:14:51.056 --> 00:14:53.525
was, like, walking out.
He didn't even make it.

00:14:53.525 --> 00:14:55.326
He was, like, walking out
to the garage.

00:14:55.326 --> 00:14:58.197
He's like, 'Where's my
old toboggan?!' Whoa!'"

00:14:58.197 --> 00:15:00.466
[ Laughter ]

00:15:02.868 --> 00:15:05.370
So I called him.

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:08.239
I called him in the hospital,
'cause he had surgery.

00:15:08.239 --> 00:15:11.043
And I called him
to check on him.

00:15:11.043 --> 00:15:12.478
And he was in --

00:15:12.478 --> 00:15:16.882
I was expecting him to be sad,
but he was in a great mood.

00:15:16.882 --> 00:15:18.751
And I said,
"I'm glad you're doing okay."

00:15:18.751 --> 00:15:21.419
And he said,
"Okay? Okay?!

00:15:21.419 --> 00:15:25.057
I'm about to be rolling in it!"

00:15:25.057 --> 00:15:28.260
And often -- the way I often
am when I talk to him --

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:32.531
I was so confused by that,
and I said,

00:15:32.531 --> 00:15:34.933
"Why?" What do you mean you're
gonna be rolling in it? Why?"

00:15:34.933 --> 00:15:36.701
And he goes,
"You know how much you make

00:15:36.701 --> 00:15:38.404
in a slip-and-fall settlement?"

00:15:42.674 --> 00:15:46.411
I said, "Yeah,
but it happened at your house."

00:15:46.411 --> 00:15:49.614
And he said, "What of it?"

00:15:49.614 --> 00:15:52.884
I go, "Who are you gonna sue?"

00:15:52.884 --> 00:15:56.088
And then it was
just really quiet

00:15:56.088 --> 00:15:57.723
for like a really long time,

00:15:57.723 --> 00:15:59.891
and I knew he was still on the
phone because he has this way

00:15:59.891 --> 00:16:02.961
he breathes
when he's real angry.

00:16:02.961 --> 00:16:04.663
And then I said,
"Are you still there?"

00:16:04.663 --> 00:16:06.765
And he goes,
"[Bleep] bobsledders."

00:16:06.765 --> 00:16:09.534
[ Laughter ]

00:16:09.534 --> 00:16:13.404
Making people think they
can do things they can't do!"

00:16:13.404 --> 00:16:16.474
But he's right.

00:16:16.474 --> 00:16:19.544
We miss him,
and he wouldn't have done it

00:16:19.544 --> 00:16:21.947
if it wasn't
for those bobsledders.

00:16:25.284 --> 00:16:28.187
And he can't take his vengeance
right now because he's laid up.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:33.859
But I know someone who can.
[ Laughs ]

00:16:33.859 --> 00:16:36.395
[ Laughter ]

00:16:43.335 --> 00:16:45.738
This has been
a "Corrections Look."

